writing:{
 title:'Task 1 – Bar chart (câu phức, band 6.5)',
 prompt:'The chart below shows the percentage of a city’s budget spent on five areas in 2005 and 2025. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant. Write at least 150 words.',
 chart:{type:'bar',title:'City budget by area (% of total spending)',unit:'%',series:['2005','2025'],cats:['Education','Health','Transport','Public safety','Parks & culture'],data:[[24,22],[15,21],[28,19],[18,20],[5,12]]},
 min:150,max:200,
 wex:[
  ['Overview tốt nhất:',['Overall, education was 24% in 2005.','Overall, the most notable changes were the fall in transport spending and the rise in health and parks, while transport lost its position as the largest area.','Overall, the city spent money.'],1,'Hai thay đổi lớn nhất + vị trí cao nhất, không số.'],
  ['Câu bị động đúng:',['28% of the budget was allocated to transport in 2005.','28% of the budget allocated to transport in 2005.','28% of the budget was allocate to transport.'],0,'was + V3.'],
  ['Câu có which (không xác định) đúng:',['Spending on parks, which more than doubled, reached 12%.','Spending on parks which more than doubled reached 12%.','Spending on parks, that more than doubled, reached 12%.'],0,'Dấu phẩy + which.'],
  ['“Gấp hơn hai lần” là:',['more than doubled','more than double times','twice more'],0,'more than doubled.'],
  ['Cách nhóm thân bài hợp lý:',['nhóm tăng (health, parks, safety) và nhóm giảm (transport, education)','theo thứ tự bảng chữ cái','mỗi cột một đoạn'],0,'Nhóm theo xu hướng.'],
  ['Câu so sánh tốt nhất:',['Transport was 28 and health was 15.','In 2005, the city spent almost twice as much on transport as on health (28% compared with 15%).','Transport was more.'],1,'twice as much as + số liệu.']],
 outline:['Mở bài: The bar chart compares the proportion of a city’s budget allocated to five areas in 2005 and 2025.','Overview: transport giảm mạnh, mất vị trí lớn nhất; health và parks tăng rõ; education chiếm phần lớn nhất năm 2025.','Thân bài 1 (giảm): transport 28→19% (bị động: was allocated); education giảm nhẹ 24→22% nhưng vẫn lớn nhất.','Thân bài 2 (tăng): health 15→21% (which…), parks & culture 5→12% (more than doubled), public safety tăng nhẹ 18→20%.'],
 model:'The bar chart compares the proportion of a city’s budget that was allocated to five areas in 2005 and 2025.<br><br><i>Overall</i>, the most significant changes were the sharp fall in spending on transport and the rise in spending on health and on parks and culture. While transport received the largest share in 2005, education had become the biggest area by 2025.<br><br>In 2005, 28% of the budget <b>was spent</b> on transport, almost twice as much as on health. Twenty years later, however, this figure had dropped to 19%, <b>which</b> made transport only the fourth-largest area. Education, <b>which</b> accounted for 24% of spending in 2005, fell slightly to 22%, but it still received the largest share in 2025.<br><br><i>By contrast</i>, the share <b>allocated to</b> health rose considerably from 15% to 21%. Spending on parks and culture, <b>which</b> was by far the smallest category in 2005 at only 5%, more than doubled to 12%. Meanwhile, public safety saw a modest increase, from 18% to 20%.',
 note:'Khoảng 175 từ, band 6.5–7.0. Có 3 mệnh đề which, 2 câu bị động, 1 câu so sánh “twice as much as”. Mỗi câu phức đều đúng ngữ pháp — điều quan trọng hơn số lượng.',
 checklist:['Overview: 2 thay đổi lớn nhất + nhóm lớn nhất','Ít nhất 2 câu bị động (was spent / was allocated)','Ít nhất 2 mệnh đề quan hệ đúng dấu phẩy','Nhóm theo xu hướng tăng / giảm','Kiểm tra mỗi mệnh đề có đúng 1 động từ chia thì','≥ 150 từ trong 20 phút']
},
speaking:{
 task:'Ghi âm Part 2 (2 phút) và cả 3 câu Part 3, gửi cô. Cố gắng dùng 1 câu điều kiện và 1 câu bị động trong Part 3.',
 items:[
 ['Are there many rules at your school?',"Quite a lot, yes. We have to wear uniforms, we can't use phones during lessons, and we have to be at school by quarter to seven. Most of them make sense, although some feel a bit strict."],
 ['Do you think young people follow rules more than in the past?',"I'm not sure. In some ways, young people today question rules more, but I think that's because they want to understand the reasons behind them, not because they're less responsible."],
 ['Have you ever done any volunteer work?',"Yes, last year I joined a beach clean-up in Vũng Tàu with my class. We collected dozens of bags of plastic in one morning, which was shocking but also motivating."],
 ['What facilities would you like in your neighbourhood?',"I'd love a public library with a quiet study area, because my home is quite noisy. A small park with exercise equipment would be great too."]],
 part2:{cue:'Describe a rule or law that you think is important.',bullets:['what the rule or law is','where it applies','how people react to it','and explain why you think it is important.'],
  notes:'compulsory helmet law, Vietnam, since 2007 · applies to everyone on motorbikes, incl. children · at first complaints (hot, hair), now almost everyone wears one; police fine people · saves lives, head injuries fell, shows rules work if enforced',
  model:"I'd like to talk about the law that makes it compulsory to wear a helmet on a motorbike in Vietnam. It was introduced in 2007, and it applies to everyone riding a motorbike, including passengers and children. When the law first came in, many people weren't happy about it. I remember my parents saying that helmets were hot and uncomfortable, especially in summer, and some people complained that they would ruin their hair. But the law was enforced quite strictly. Police officers stopped people who weren't wearing helmets and gave them fines, and there were lots of campaigns on TV that raised awareness of road safety. Today, almost everyone wears a helmet, and it's become a normal habit. In fact, I feel strange if I get on a motorbike without one. I think this law is important for two reasons. First, and most obviously, it saves lives. Motorbikes are the main form of transport in Vietnam, and if people didn't wear helmets, many more would suffer serious head injuries in accidents. Second, I think it shows that a rule can change behaviour if it's clearly explained and properly enforced. If the government had introduced the law without checking, I doubt it would have been so successful."},
 part3:[
 ['Why do some people break rules?',"I think the main reason is that they don't see the immediate consequences. For example, if someone runs a red light and nothing happens, they're likely to do it again. Also, some rules aren't enforced consistently, so people assume they won't be caught."],
 ['Should the government or individuals be responsible for people’s health?',"I'd say it's a shared responsibility. The government should create the right conditions, for example by regulating unhealthy food and building parks. But in the end, individuals have to make their own choices, and no law can force someone to exercise."],
 ['What would happen if there were no traffic laws?',"I think there would be chaos. If there were no rules about speed or which side of the road to drive on, accidents would increase dramatically, and journeys would take much longer because nobody would know what other drivers were going to do."]]
},
